            <ArticleTitle>A Rare Cause of Postprandial Abdominal Pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>12-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4103/2006-8808.184936</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 49-year-old woman with medical history of polymyalgia rheumatica presented with 3 weeks history of epigastric pain worse after meal. Gallstones, peptic ulcer, bowel ischemia, bowel obstruction, gastroesophageal reflux disease, chest pathology, etc., were excluded from the study. Computerized tomography showed a short stenotic segment at coeliac trunk with poststenotic dilatation of 8 mm. Ultrasound scan showed peak velocity of 326 cm/s at the coeliac artery. Diagnosis of median arcuate ligament syndrome was made.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
